that type of network is as  easy as saying this network operator dot  send and then your message if you wanted  you can decide never to place an  operator with a mouse ever you can  ignore the visual environment completely  all operators can be created or  destroyed with Python alone and on the  chart over here we have examples of some  of the ways you can modify operators via  Python and you can also modify them at  creation or after creation by addressing  them by their operator name so in this  case we have a piece of text which  creates a sphere object called my sphere  and then the second text object we have  something that destroys any object by  the name of my sphere  there are also some Python modules  automatically in touch designer that are  pretty handy store and fetch is  interesting because it's a fast and easy  way to store anything as a global  variable including an entire operator or  an entire network of operators you can  also talk to operators by their name  using me dot digits or parent digits  parent digit refers to that operators  place in the hierarchy so operators  above it and below it so this is handy  when you're using replication and you  have an unknown amount of operators or a  very large amount of operators with  python scripts attached to them and you  just want to know what the name is in  order to perform a particular function  function on the operator so save 100  noise channels and you want them to all  be unique you can make the seed of that  noise equal to it's me dot digits in  touch designer you can use as much or as  little python as you want it's equally  possible to create a project entirely  with Python as it is to create one  without you might be asking what you can  do with this and basically anything you  can hack some devices you have lying  around you can make a game you can  collect data on mass you can prototype  software for me working on the app and  SDK right now  it's much easier for me to prototype  something in a day in touch designer  than spend two weeks doing it in Xcode  if I want to find out how if a game  mechanic is fun or not I can invest a  little bit less time improve it before I  spend all that time building it you can  also turn your data skills into art and  you can design and execute complete  science experiments and you can make  something that's a little bit of both  I'm not going to talk about a project I  worked on with some brain scientists  that share it a here in Berlin and  Baycrest home in Toronto my virtual  dream is both in our installation and a  science experiment my virtual dream is a  collective neurofeedback experience  where four subjects could control layers  of video with their brain and also music  I worked on the BCI I worked with a BCI  team to make something that was  simultaneously a collective interactive  experience but also a novel way to study  the brain don't make a noise in my  virtual dream 4 subjects were fitted  with commercial EEG headsets we wanted  to collect data to see if people learned  how to control their brain state either  relax or focus within the short time  frame of 20 minutes we also wanted to  test and verify if neurofeedback could  be used as a reliable input did the  subjects feel in control of the dream  and how does the data compare here  gathered in the wild as it does the data  that we collect in the lab  you might recognize this from the first  video this is my control panel for my  virtual dream I can monitor the subjects  and the progress of the software and  just in case something breaks change the  settings in my case this is the top  layer of my network and all the guts are  underneath the first challenge was  getting the brain data from the headsets  the brain computer interface team first  acquired the data from the headset and  determined the state of the subjects  which was where they were in a scale of  relaxation to focus this was pre  processed and sent to me over network  I'm using a replicator here because I  designed the software to include any  number of subjects we settled on for  later but we could change this in the  future here I organized and defined the  data and what to do with the data a  player is defined by their score their  color the video of music that they  control I can also define if they are on  a team in the case of large volumes of  players in the past we had up to 20 and  we've discussed going up to 100 players  have momentary an accumulative measure  momentary is their immediate state at  this moment which fluctuates wildly it's  hard to control cumulative as a second  measure which is a description of their  state in general more points are awarded  to cumulative if the more they maintain  a relaxed or focused state both of these  these measures are used for the players  to tell them about how they're doing and  to give them constant feedback when you  zoom out of the network it looks like  this I think of it as a machine because  I can swap out parts at any time I can  make new components which change the  entire behavior of the experiment and I  can take components from this experiment  and make new experiments it's organized  by function and it's color-coded there  are different areas of the network for  connections player identity how data is  saved how the audio and video assets are  classified and how different behaviors  of the program work and interact with  each other the  second challenge was graphics I wanted  to keep it modular and scalable I wanted  to be able to add more scenes and  animations easily and I wanted to be  able to do the same for the music I  wanted everything to reflect the state  of the player and I wanted to control  everything with Python scripts to make  it automatic here's an example of a bank  of dream images that were used to make  the entire presentation each component  contains all the animation layers and  all the special behaviors of each scene  they're given an ID and a classification  and tables so that I can easily refer to  them anywhere in the project and grab a  dream by its name or type after all of  that I was able to write this script  this is the dream selection logic and  it's called every frame when I need a  new dream this happens maybe two or four  anywhere between two and four times for  each set of subjects for a study to know  the mood of the dreamers this is an  amalgamation of all the subject states  which is either relaxed focused or  somewhere in between  then a set is made of all possible  dreams which fit into this category  after this I subtract the dreams which  have already been played so I don't get  repeats and then I select a dream from  random from that set and add that to the  dream setlist so that it doesn't get  played again and returned the idea of  that dream operators which handle dream  scenes know them by their ID so I can  cue them with the script script anywhere  in the project and when it all comes  together like this it feels like magic  the entire 20-minute experience for the  four subjects is triggered by a series  of daisy chain timers I remember when I  showed you the event handlers on the  timer chop where you can put Python  instructions it's basically this a lot I  did this so that I could edit the length  of the scenes from a single table to  adjust how the whole experience played  out maybe I wanted it much shorter or  maybe I wanted the intro to be really  long so that someone could give a talk  at the beginning or maybe I just want it  to run really quickly so I could debug  it after the dream is selected it's  cross blended with the previous one and  here I made a custom crossfade component  since I wanted something fancy it then  goes through a bunch of special effects  processing and then it gets added to one  video still at the end of the network  funneling down and this is happy all of  this stuff is happening 60 times a  second here's a snippet from one of the  timers when the timer it's done it will  execute the next stream and trigger the  crossfade there's also a function called  shriek and shriek is a special function  close to my heart this leads to the  third channel this is a challenge this  is a science experiment and we want to  look at the data after raw data is being  saved by the brain computer interface  team but they want markers from me so  they can go through the data later and  find out what the subjects were  experiencing when the data was being  saved I also want to be able to look at  the text log so that can read what  happened and recreate dreams just from  text I also want to monitor the  experience while it's happening shriek  is an elegant solution or my elegant  solution it does everything at the same  time I can send marks over Network so  that the BC I can log it on their  computer and it also saves the events to  my computer and possibly it could do  other things at the same time people  wanted to change the marks all the time  so tables again was the easiest solution  on the Left we just have the index for  every event that could happen during the  experiment and the second one is the  human readable string that I wanted and  the last one is the mark that the BCI  team wanted a colleague Carol eNOS  Toshio has taken the data collected from  MBD and started to interpret it there's  still a lot to work to be done but in  this graph it depicts the subjects  ability to use a single type metric  relaxation versus the absence of  relaxation and the purple depict depicts  the subjects ability to use a dual type  metric which is relaxation versus focus  which are not mutually exclusive which  may explain why it's more difficult  there's still lots to learn and it will  probably never end and I hope that this  talked helped you imagine how you might  use touch designer to make your own  experiments a great place to learn more  and probably all you ever need to know  are the tutorials of matthew regan  there's also lots of really great  documentation and a place to start with  touch designer on their wiki  I can talk about this all day every day  so if you have any questions you can  send me an email or find me on LinkedIn  also for a long night of the sciences  we're going to do a demonstration of MBD  and our brain modes app and dr. Petra  Ritter will be giving talks on the  virtual brain which is an open source  full brain simulator thanks for  listening and enjoy the rest of the  great talks at PI data very much for  this interesting talk and now will we  have time for any questions okay um I'll  start can you please I'm talking if it  is it like open source or how can you um  get this the fancy software you can get  it for free to use right away but the  resolution is capped and it is missing  like a few features but you can  basically do everything that I just  showed you in the free version I think  the educational version costs around 300  which will allow you to use the software  and events where you're accepting money  payments and then there's like a super  fancy fancy version which costs about  two thousand that you can lock your  network so that no one can see what you  did in the case that you want to take  what you did and sell it to like a  museum or another laboratory or  something and you want to protect your  IP but basically it's free for messing  around  the headset that we're using is called  amused by a company called interaction  and it's a four-channel eg with two  grounding sensors in the front so it's  very minimal and crude and this costs  about 300 Canadian dollars I think which  is basically pennies and euros I think  and but I think the most capable EG  headset available for commercial use is  the Emotiv EPOC which is a 16 channel  headset and I believe just normal this  costs about 400 euros but if you want to  have like access to all the EEG you have  to fork out for the SDK and we're  running about 700 you can also make your  own you can I think it's called open BCI  and you can get instructions on how to  make your own from scratch okay thank  you very much for listening  and that's breathe tank um Jessica yeah
 

